// Memory corruption patterns in V8 compiler

// 1. Heap corruption detection
// compiler/heap-refs.cc:1117-1121:
//   "for Maps: in GetMapInstanceType we have special handling for maps and will
//   report MAP_TYPE for objects whose map pointer points back to itself. With
//   heap corruption, a non-map object can be made to point to itself though,
//   in which case we may later treat a non-MapData object as a MapData object.
//   See also crbug.com/326700497 for more details."

// 2. Out-of-bounds access patterns
// compiler/escape-analysis.h:132-136:
//   "CHECK(!HasEscaped());
//   if (offset >= size()) {
//     // TODO(turbofan): Reading out-of-bounds can only happen in unreachable
//     // code. In this case, we have to mark the object as escaping to avoid
//     // dead nodes in the graph. This is a workaround that should be removed"
//   }

// 3. Out-of-bounds detection in code generation
// compiler/backend/arm64/code-generator-arm64.cc:2331:
//   "because INT32_MIN allows easier out-of-bounds detection."
// compiler/backend/arm64/code-generator-arm64.cc:2353:
//   "because 0 allows easier out-of-bounds detection."
// compiler/backend/arm64/code-generator-arm64.cc:2387:
//   "because INT64_MIN allows easier out-of-bounds detection."

// 4. DataView out-of-bounds handling
// compiler/js-call-reducer.cc:8682-8685:
//   "if (m.HasResolvedValue() && m.Ref(broker).IsJSDataView()) {
//     // We only deal with DataViews here whose [[ByteLength]] is at least
//     // {element_size}, as for all other DataViews it'll be out-of-bounds."

// 5. Instruction selector out-of-bounds memory access
// compiler/backend/instruction-selector.cc:1481-1486:
//   "On x64 there exists an optimization that folds
//   `kF64x2PromoteLowF32x4` and `kS128Load64Zero` together into a single
//   instruction. If the instruction causes an out-of-bounds memory
//   access exception, then the stack trace has to show the source
//   position of the `kS128Load64Zero` and not of the"

// 6. Turboshaft out-of-bounds handling
// compiler/turboshaft/operations.h:2927:
//   "There is a Wasm trap handler for out-of-bounds accesses."

// 7. Growing sidetable out-of-bounds
// compiler/turboshaft/sidetable.h:23-26:
//   "This sidetable is a conceptually infinite mapping from Turboshaft operation
//   indices to values. It grows automatically and default-initializes the table
//   when accessed out-of-bounds."
